ask: Physical media as computation readouts: the water-surface model-size/dither/ripples analogy (kwargq), our water-graph XOR/SILL junction gates, and the hydro_xor_sim two-bucket XOR. Can a real water computer perform useful computation? What's the state of fluidic logic? Return papers and builds.

---

(1) FINDING: Fluidic logic is physically real and was industrially explored in the 1960s-70s using air or water jets for amplification and switching. Water-based computation models can demonstrate logic topology but lack noise margins and speed for practical general-purpose computing.

(2) STEALABLE idea: Use a hydrostatic pressure XOR junction as a fault-tolerant sensor fusion gate in submerged or explosive environments where electrical sparks are prohibited.

(3) TESTABLE hypothesis: A two-bucket water XOR with gravity return will toggle reliably at input flow rates above 50 mL/min and settle to a stable output within 3 seconds of input removal at 20C +/- 1C.

(4) EVIDENCE or OWED: OWED. The specific references to kwargq water-surface models, water-graph XOR/SILL junction gates, and hydro_xor_sim builds could not be verified. General fluidic logic literature exists in IEEE Transactions on Fluid Electronics circa 1965-1975 but exact titles are not cited here to avoid fabrication.
